I reviewed perf2/receipt-rate-control (41272e3) against perf2/viewer-feedback in /Users/lb7666/rust_pro/pb-mapper/.claude/worktrees/wf_352d1379-280-59. I read the brief, R8 and its critique. I left the branch unchanged and deleted target/ afterwards.

**What I ran here (Apple M6, macOS 27.0, Rust 1.99.1)**
- `cargo test -p pb-mapper-desktop --lib qos::`: 47 passed.
- `cargo test -p pb-mapper-desktop --all-features`: 252 lib tests passed, 27 ignored, and the integration tests passed.
- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`: clean.
- `cargo clippy -p pb-mapper-desktop --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`: clean.
- One session-wiring mutation and two temporary qos/sim.rs cases, all reverted afterwards.
- Not re-run: the release slow_link suite and host_send_bench. I checked the implementer's raw files against the README instead; they match, environment included.

**Design steps (brief items 1-13)**
- All present, with one exception: the `video_bench --rate-steps` device tool does not exist. The implementer reported this.
- The listed deviations are explained and reasonable:
  - the receipt-local rebase level;
  - the credit ledger reading the rebase;
  - the recovery re-arm;
  - stall and drain refinements;
  - the rule that a cut within 5% of the asked rate only holds.
- Every step checks against the code:
  - cut order, including Quality's;
  - the pace cap applied before the startup, unresponsive and JPEG clamps;
  - the owned arm below 600 ms;
  - the rebase offset (capped at 150 ms) and its clears;
  - FAST_START removed, except for JPEG;
  - the 3 s step skipped while receipts come;
  - the resets;
  - `blocked`, set only by a clamped window with no IDR in flight;
  - the episode window clamp, with no qos/credit lock nesting;
  - trace fields;
  - no wire, helper-pipe or FFI change;
  - no allocation per receipt.

**Records and conventions**
- DECISIONS D5 and CONTRACT-perf R13 record the deviation and the owner's directive.
- remote-desktop.md has no Chinese pair, so no pair update was needed.
- CLAUDE.md conventions hold: no new error types, no environment writes, no Flutter change.

**Tests**
- The slow_link before-runs fail moderate_limit_keeps_60, sudden_drop and recurring_stalls_cut, so those cases discriminate.
- The sim and unit tests cover the controller's logic well. The session wiring is the exception (finding 2).

**Main issue (finding 1)**
On a light picture, the fps cap set by a receipt cut is lifted only by the usage-gated raise. Two temporary sim cases show the session stuck at 42 or 13 FPS, with bits per frame at the floor, for 40 s or more after the link is clear. In the second case the cut came from a 60 ms queue the host did not cause. On the base branch the probe controller kept 60 FPS in both cases.

**Needs a device or CI**
- Windows host and helper build (rust-windows-desktop) and rust-macos-desktop.
- nat-traversal.yml, which needs Linux and sudo.
- How MFT and VideoToolbox encoders follow bitrate changes up to four times a second.
- Real Wi-Fi, cellular and relay traces.

